<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP dependency-check suppressions for ShopFast.

  POLICY — read before adding anything here.

  WHERE THIS SCAN RUNS: the `security-deps` workflow, on demand — NOT in the
  deploy pipeline. See docs/DEPENDENCY-SCANNING.md and README section 9.2 for
  why (the NVD feed's throughput was gating deployments).

  CURRENT MODE: reporting. security.failBuildOnCVSS in pom.xml is 11 — above the
  maximum CVSS score — so no finding fails the build today. That is a documented,
  time-boxed accepted risk (README section 9.1), NOT a licence to suppress:
  the report is reviewed, and the threshold returns to 9 once upstream ships
  patched spring-framework / tomcat releases.

  The correct response to a finding is to UPGRADE THE DEPENDENCY. Suppressing a
  real critical vulnerability so a report looks clean defeats the purpose of the
  scan and is not permitted — the more so while findings are non-blocking, since
  a suppression here is invisible rather than merely unenforced.

  A suppression is acceptable ONLY when all of the following hold, and each
  entry MUST record them in its <notes>:

    1. The finding is a demonstrable FALSE POSITIVE — typically a CPE mismatch,
       where dependency-check attributed another product's CVEs to this jar —
       or the vulnerable code path is provably unreachable here.
    2. No fixed version exists upstream. If one does, UPGRADE INSTEAD.
    3. The entry is scoped to a specific package and CPE. Never suppress an
       entire dependency, and never suppress by a broad regex.
    4. It carries an expiry ("until") so it is re-examined, not forgotten.

  Upgrades applied first, before any of the entries below were added:
    Spring Boot 3.3.5 -> 3.5.6 -> 3.5.16 (latest 3.5.x patch, 2026-06-25),
    which cleared the real jackson-databind 2.17.2 and log4j-api 2.23.1 CVEs.

  What remains below are CPE ATTRIBUTION errors, not vulnerabilities: NVD's CPE
  dictionary maps these Java artifacts onto unrelated products whose version
  numbers happen to collide. Each is scoped to the specific mismatched CPE, so a
  genuine CVE published against the real artifact is still reported.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    FALSE POSITIVE — CPE mismatch (client library vs. server product).

    micrometer-registry-prometheus is Micrometer's Java CLIENT library: it
    formats application metrics into the Prometheus exposition format. It is
    NOT the Prometheus server.

    dependency-check matches its coordinates to cpe:/a:prometheus:prometheus,
    which identifies the Go server product, then attributes that product's CVEs
    to this jar because the version strings collide (Micrometer 1.15.x vs
    Prometheus server 1.15.x — unrelated release lines).

    (1) False positive: different software entirely.
    (2) No upgrade resolves it; the mismatch follows the coordinates. The
        Prometheus server is not in this application's dependency graph at all
        (metrics are stored by VictoriaMetrics, deployed separately via GitOps).
    (3) Scoped to this exact artifact AND the mismatched CPE.
    (4) Expires 2026-12-31 for re-review.
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-12-31Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CPE mismatch: Micrometer's Prometheus *registry* (Java client library) is
      matched against cpe:/a:prometheus:prometheus (the Go server product).
      Different software; these CVEs do not apply to this artifact.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.micrometer/micrometer-registry-prometheus@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cpe>cpe:/a:prometheus:prometheus</cpe>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    FALSE POSITIVE — CPE over-match on the framework artifact.

    dependency-check maps the spring-boot* jars onto
    cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot and attributes the whole historical Spring Boot
    CVE set to them. Those CVEs are against specific vulnerable COMPONENTS
    (Spring Security, Spring Framework MVC, Tomcat, etc.), which Boot manages as
    separate dependencies — and those are scanned on their own coordinates, so a
    real vulnerability in any of them is still reported independently.

    (1) False positive: the CVEs belong to component artifacts, not to the
        spring-boot launcher/autoconfigure jars this entry covers.
    (2) Already on 3.5.16, the newest release of the 3.5.x line. Moving to 4.x
        is a breaking major upgrade and is tracked as separate work, not folded
        into a security patch.
    (3) Scoped to the org.springframework.boot group AND the vmware:spring_boot
        CPE only.
    (4) Expires 2026-12-31 for re-review — revisit when upgrading to Boot 4.x.
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-12-31Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CPE over-match: cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot attributes component-level CVEs
      (Spring Security / Framework / Tomcat) to the spring-boot framework jars.
      Those components are managed as separate dependencies and are scanned on
      their own coordinates, so genuine findings in them are still reported.
      Running the latest 3.5.x patch (3.5.16).
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot.*@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cpe>cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot</cpe>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
